<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="Learning-Outcomes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Learning Outcomes</title>
    <introduction>
        <p> By the end of this course, you should be able to:
      </p>
      <p>
        Name: ____________________ &#160;&#160;
        #: ____________________
      </p>
    </introduction>

  <subsection>
    <title>Vectors</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>V1</cell><cell>Add vectors.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>V2</cell><cell>Scale vectors by a real number.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>V3</cell><cell>Calculate the length of a vector.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>V4</cell><cell>Compute the dot product between two vectors.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>V5</cell><cell>Calculate the angle between two given vectors.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>V6</cell><cell>Find the orthogonal projection of one vector onto another.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Systems of Equations</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SE1</cell><cell>Identify whether a matrix is in Reduced Row Echelon Form.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SE2</cell><cell>Use Gaussian Elimination to put a matrix into Reduced Row Echelon Form.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SE3</cell><cell>Use the Reduced Row Echelon Form of an augmented matrix to describe the solution space of a system of linear equations.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SE4</cell><cell>Use pivot positions to determine whether a linear system is consistent.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SE5</cell><cell>Determine whether the solution to a consistent linear system is unique.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SE6</cell><cell>Determine the rank of a matrix from its Reduced Row Echelon Form.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Matrices</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>M1</cell><cell>Add matrices.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>M2</cell><cell>Scale matrices by a real number.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>M3</cell><cell>Perform matrix-vector multiplication.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>M4</cell><cell>Perform matrix-matrix multiplication.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>M5</cell><cell>Compute the transpose of a matrix.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>M6</cell><cell>Translate a system of equations into a matrix equation <m>Ax=b</m> and the vector equation form.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Span and Linear Independence</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SLI1</cell><cell>Determine whether a vector can be expressed as a linear combination of given vectors.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SLI2</cell><cell>Determine whether a vector is in the span of a given set of vectors.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SLI3</cell><cell>Describe the span of a set of vectors using set notation.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SLI4</cell><cell>Use span to determine whether a linear system is consistent.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>SLI5</cell><cell>Determine whether a given set of vectors is linearly independent.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Matrix Transformations</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>MT1</cell><cell>Determine if a transformation is linear.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>MT2</cell><cell>Find the domain and range of a matrix transformation.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>MT3</cell><cell>Compose matrix transformations.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>MT4</cell><cell>Find the standard matrix of a matrix transformation.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Invertibility and Determinants</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>ID1</cell><cell>Use Gaussian Elimination to determine whether a matrix is invertible.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>ID2</cell><cell>Find the inverse of an invertible matrix.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>ID3</cell><cell>Compute the determinant of a matrix.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>ID4</cell><cell>Use the determinant to determine whether a matrix is invertible.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>ID5</cell><cell>Analyze determinants of products and inverses of matrices.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Vector Spaces and Bases</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>VSB1</cell><cell>Determine whether a set satisfies the definition of a vector space.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>VSB2</cell><cell>Find a basis for an abstract vector space.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>VSB3</cell><cell>Translate between coordinate systems for different bases.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>VSB4</cell><cell>Find a basis for the null space of a matrix.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>VSB5</cell><cell>Find a basis for the column space of a matrix.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Eigentheory</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>E1</cell><cell>Determine whether a vector is an eigenvector of a matrix.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>E2</cell><cell>Find the eigenvalue associated with an eigenvector.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>E3</cell><cell>Use the characteristic polynomial to find eigenvalues.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>E4</cell><cell>Find the algebraic and geometric multiplicities of eigenvalues.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>E5</cell><cell>Find a basis for an eigenspace.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>E6</cell><cell>Determine whether a matrix is diagonalizable.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>E7</cell><cell>Diagonalize a diagonalizable matrix.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Orthogonality</title>

    <table>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><em>Done</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Code</em></cell>
          <cell><em>Learning Outcome</em></cell>
        </row>

        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>O1</cell><cell>Determine whether a given map is an inner product.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>O2</cell><cell>Determine whether two vectors from an abstract vector space are orthogonal.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>O3</cell><cell>Use the transpose to find a basis for the orthogonal complement of a vector space.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>O4</cell><cell>Perform orthogonal decomposition.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>O5</cell><cell>Use Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization to find an orthogonal basis.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\square</m></cell><cell>O6</cell><cell>Find an orthonormal basis from an orthogonal basis.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
</subsection>
</section>
